// Running something, with less authority than you have.
//
// This is the one place in the tree where authority is **handed on** rather than held, and it is
// where "authority is a value" meets the thing it cannot cross.
//
// ## What the real platform does, which is already right
//
// `std/platform.wac` takes grants as bit flags and says why they are a request:
//
//   > The host intersects them with its own grants, so these are a request rather than an
//   > instruction and a parent can never widen what it was given.
//
// That property is the whole of capability attenuation and it is already enforced. Nothing in this
// file improves on it.
//
// ## The part vision changes, and the part it cannot
//
// Within one instance, authority is a value: a function that was handed a narrower `Sys` cannot
// reach past it, and that is checked by the type rather than intersected by a host.
//
// **Across a spawn it is not, and cannot be.** `std/platform.wac`: *"A spawned child is a separate
// instance with its own memory."* A reference does not cross an instance boundary, so a `Sys` value
// cannot be handed to a child — what crosses is a description of one. The bitfield is not a design
// compromise, it is the serialised form of a capability, and any model in which authority is a
// value needs a second thing that is the wire format of that value.
//
// `SHOWCASE.md` already writes the second thing — `sys.spawn(wasm, [], [Grant.Read])` — and nowhere
// says it is a different kind of thing from the `Sys` two lines above it. That is the gap: the two
// look alike and only one of them can be sent.
//
// ## And the gap is widening, which this file did not notice
//
// The wire format has two dimensions — a category and a root, `GRANT_READ | GRANT_NET` plus a `dir`,
// which `std/platform.wac` sums up as *"a shell served over a socket can be given one directory and
// no network."* Both are things a host can enforce.
//
// The value has more than two, now. `vision/packages/fs` makes a `Files` a struct of closures, so it
// can be a memory tree, a host subtree, a read-only wrapper, an overlay of one over another, or a
// fake — and there is no `GRANT_` for *reads fall through to this and writes land in that*. The
// flag set is not short by one; the value's expressiveness is open-ended and the wire's is closed,
// and every proposal that makes a capability more expressive inside an instance makes more of it
// unsendable.
//
// So: **a capability narrowed structurally survives a call and dies at a spawn; one narrowed
// categorically survives both.** Nothing says that, and the failure it describes is silent — a
// parent hands a child `GRANT_READ` believing it passed on its own narrowed view, and the child gets
// the process's. `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has the three ways out.

import { Result, Ticket, Bytes, Vec } from "core";
import { Proc, NotGranted } from "std";

/**
 * What a child may do — the serialisable form of authority.
 *
 * An enum rather than bit flags because a caller writes what it means and a `match` over it is
 * checked, and because adding a member does not need a `GRANT_ALL` kept in step by hand — which
 * `std/platform.wac` names as the cost of the fourth spelling.
 *
 * There is deliberately nothing for standard input and output. A child always has those; they are
 * how a parent talks to it at all, and they reach nothing the parent did not hand over.
 */
/**
 * The categories — and **`Env` and `Run` are not independent, which this enum asserts they are**.
 *
 * Found 2026-09-04 in a comment on `packages/wactest/src/childenv.wac`, of all places:
 * *"`issues/system/0198` is the half that is still open: inheritance is an authority nobody
 * declared."* `std/platform.wac` states the mechanism at `execWithIn`'s `clearEnv` parameter:
 *
 * > False — what `Cli.exec` passes — *adds* `env` to everything this process holds, and that
 * > inheritance is an authority nobody declared: **a program refused `--allow-env` can still read
 * > `HOME` by running `printenv`, so `--allow-run` confers `--allow-env`.**
 *
 * So the shipped design has one grant conferring another, by default, and knows it: `clearEnv` is
 * the fix, *"not the default **yet**, and the reason is a count rather than a doubt: 342 call sites"*.
 * An open issue against a live default, with the sweep sized.
 *
 * **In `vision` it is worse than a default, because there is no parameter.** `Proc.spawn` is
 * `(Bytes wasm, Vec<Bytes> argv, Vec<Grant> want)` — no environment, so a child cannot be given one
 * and cannot be denied one. Whatever the host does is what happens, which is the definition of
 * ambient, in the one call that creates a program. Listing `Env` beside `Run` then claims a
 * separation the signature cannot deliver.
 *
 * This is the exercise's central claim meeting a filed issue against it. *No ambient capabilities* is
 * enforced within an instance — a function without a `Files` cannot read — and at the instance
 * boundary the environment crosses because nobody said it should not. `@/packages/box`'s finding was
 * that authority is per-instance and the language's per-function narrowing stops at the module edge;
 * this is the same edge from the other side, and here the leak has a name and a number.
 *
 * The fix is one more parameter and the question is what shape. `clearEnv`'s bool plus an
 * `NAME=value` list is the shipped answer and its own doc says what it still cannot do: *"Removing a
 * named variable is still not expressible … a caller who wants everything except one name has to
 * name what stays, and nothing here can enumerate the environment."* Which makes the environment the
 * one part of a grant that is neither a category nor a root — `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s *two
 * dimensions* entry has room for a third and does not have one.
 */
export enum Grant {
  Read,       // readFile, stat, readDir, openInput on a path
  Write,      // writeFile, mkdir, remove, rename, openOutput to a path
  Net,        // the child's own sockets, not the parent's
  Env,        // env, which reports every variable unset without it
  Run         // exec — the one authority the narrowing exists for
}

/**
 * Start `wasm` with at most `want`.
 *
 * **At most.** The host intersects `want` with what this program holds, so asking for `Run` from a
 * program that has not got it is not an error and not a refusal — it is a child without `Run`. A
 * request rather than an instruction, which is what makes a chain of spawns monotonically narrowing
 * however careless the middle of it is.
 *
 * It takes a `Proc` rather than a `Sys` because starting something is all it does. Handing it the
 * whole grant would let a shell's spawn path read a file, and the narrowing that this function is
 * *about* would not apply to the function itself.
 */
// **This was a free function taking a `Proc`**, which made the projection a token proving authority
// rather than the interface exercising it — the only one of the seven used that way, and not a
// decision anybody took. `Proc.spawn` is a method now; what is left here is the `Grant` enum and the
// argument about what can cross, which is this file's actual subject.
//
// The signature it had:
//
//     export async Result<Child, NotGranted> spawn(Proc proc, Bytes wasm, Vec<Bytes> args,
//                                                  Vec<Grant> want)
//
// and the paragraph above it — *"It takes a `Proc` rather than a `Sys` because starting something is
// all it does"* — was arguing for the narrowing while writing the shape that gives it up. A `Proc`
// that only proves you may spawn is a `Proc` any holder can spawn *with*, through any function that
// asks for one, which is what a capability is supposed to stop.

/** Funcrefs, for `vision/core/ticket.wac`'s reason — and a spawned child is a thing a test fakes. */
export struct Child {
  fn<Ticket<Result<i32,  NotGranted>>()>       exit;
  fn<Ticket<Result<void, NotGranted>>(Bytes)>  send;
}
